Both files are below. I haven't built or run them. The previous attempt trapped because it called `expect`/`panic!` when the `urls` key was missing or held nothing parseable. This version never panics. It tries the keys `urls`, `subscriptions` and `feeds`, and it accepts newline-, comma- or whitespace-separated values as well as a JSON array of strings. It keeps only tokens that look like URLs (they contain `://`).

If no key yields a URL, it returns an empty list rather than inventing one. If your `present(urls)` check requires a non-empty list, that case would still fail it, but the contract gives me nothing legitimate to put there.

